<h2>A century of devolution in one khata</h2>
<p>The facts span five generations. Mauji Ram died leaving his widow Khajano and a son, Ram Chander; the son died in 1937, and under the law then applicable Khajano took a limited estate — the classic Hindu widow\'s estate, enjoyable for life with reversion to her husband\'s male line. The Delhi Land Reforms Act came into force on 20 July 1954, and Khajano was declared Bhumidhar. Two years later the Hindu Succession Act transformed the legal landscape: by Section 14(1), property possessed by a female Hindu, whether acquired before or after the Act, is held by her as full owner and not as a limited owner. Khajano remarried, had two sons, and died in 1973. When those sons sought mutation in 1997, the reversioners objected — and the litigation that followed passed through the SDM, two revenue appeals, a single judge of the Delhi High Court (who sided with the reversioners in 2004) and a Division Bench in LPA No. 92 of 2005 (which reversed in 2012), before reaching the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>The interpretive question: when is personal law "frozen"?</h2>
<p>The reversioners\' strongest argument was temporal. The DLR Act was enacted in 1954, when Khajano\'s interest was still a limited estate; succession provisions, they said, should be read with the personal law as it stood at the Act\'s commencement. The Court rejected the submission on the text of Section 51(2)(a) itself: the provision speaks of the woman who "dies", a present-tense event, so the personal law applicable on the date of death controls. The Court put it crisply — the personal law is not frozen on the enactment of the 1954 Act. Reliance on <em>Bajaya v. Gopikabai</em>, a three-judge decision treating similar references to personal law as general referential incorporation that picks up later statutory changes, closed the point.</p>

<h2>The two limbs compared</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>Section 51(2)(a)(i) — limited holder</h3><p>The woman held the land as a limited or widow\'s estate under the old Hindu law. On her death the holding reverts to the heirs of the last male holder — the reversioners\' route, under the Section 50 order of succession.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>Section 51(2)(a)(ii) — absolute holder</h3><p>The woman held an absolute interest under her personal law as in force at her death. The holding devolves on her own heirs under the Section 53 table — the route the Court held applicable, because Section 14(1) HSA had enlarged Khajano\'s estate by 1973.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What the decision settles</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer after Sultan Singh</th></tr>
<tr><td>Which date fixes the applicable personal law under Section 51?</td><td>The date of the holder\'s death, not the DLR Act\'s commencement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Does Section 14(1) HSA operate on Bhumidhari rights?</td><td>Yes — a widow\'s limited estate in possession ripened into absolute ownership</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can reversioners claim holdings of widows dying after 1956?</td><td>Generally not, where the widow possessed the holding at the HSA\'s commencement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Status of the Delhi High Court Division Bench ruling of 2012</td><td>Upheld; the appeal was dismissed with no order as to costs</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes for Delhi village-land matters</h2>
<p>Mutation and partition disputes over agricultural khatas in villages such as Bhawana often outlive the parties who began them — this appeal itself was fourteen years old, and the underlying mutation dates to 1997. Three working points emerge. First, establish the chain: date of the male holder\'s death, the widow\'s possession in 1956, and her date of death — these facts, provable from jamabandis and mutation records, decide which limb of Section 51(2)(a) governs. Second, remember that remarriage did not defeat the widow\'s enlarged estate here; the devolution followed her heirs, including sons of the second marriage. Third, revenue findings travel: the litigation route runs from the SDM through the Financial Commissioner to the High Court under Article 226 and in LPA, so the record built at the earliest stage is usually the record argued twenty years later.</p>

